            <ArticleTitle>Clinical rationale for tooth stabilization and splinting.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1-6, v</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Tooth mobility can be a consequence of periodontal inflammation, attachment loss, and occlusal trauma. At times, this mobility may be tolerable, whereas at other times mobility may contribute to occlusal instability and/or patient discomfort. This article describes the clinical rationale for and the effects of splinting mobile teeth.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
